{"text":"Recent work supports separating persistent preferences from transient intent. [MISER (2024)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.knosys.2024.112657) combines multiple interests, a time-period module for short-term changes, and contrastive learning to distinguish interests. [Hierarchical long/short-term modeling (online 2025)](https://journal.hep.com.cn/fcs/EN/10.1007/s11704-025-41181-y) uses dynamic routing to extract multiple long-term vectors, self-attention for recent intent, and similarity matching plus time encoding to fuse them. These challenge a single-vector representation, although multi-interest extraction alone does not establish when an intent expires.

[HyperHawkes (WWW 2025)](https://evazangerle.at/publication/peintner-www-2025/peintner-www-2025.pdf) is especially relevant: hypergraphs and soft clustering infer latent intents from interactions; Hawkes processes model their temporal recurrence, while attention mixtures capture recent item-level interests. Its temporal component models recurrence as well as decay, rather than treating every old interest as permanently obsolete. [Elastic Multi-intent Adaptation (2024)](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10400839/) explicitly removes inactive intent vectors under memory constraints. More recently, [DSRec (September 2026 preprint)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21548) separates historical preferences and local intent into two state-space branches, with inter-click gaps modulating short-term state evolution.

Practical synthesis for SASRec—an architectural hypothesis, not a reported result: retain several persistent interest vectors alongside a recent-intent branch; learn intent-specific decay or retention gates from observed time gaps, recurrence, and recent compatibility. Permit reactivation when related interactions return. Infer relevance from histories rather than assigning ownership or satisfied-need labels. At prediction time, anchor elapsed-time features to the last observed interaction unless the evaluation contract supplies a query timestamp. Validate whether these additions improve macro HR@10 over simpler recency pooling."}
